\chapter{Creating native libraries}
A native library is a record registered under a name. The record contains
functions, constants, or nested records. A \api{Package.mull} entry point
retrieves it with \api{G:Native}. This avoids a separate foreign-function
signature language and lets the host compiler check the wrapper.

\section{C++ registration}
\begin{code}
mull::Value::Object api;
api["Double"] = mull::Value(mull::Value::Native(
    [](const mull::Value::List &args) {
        mull::arity(args, 1);
        return mull::Value(args[0].number() * 2);
    }));
vm.registerLibrary("example", mull::Value(api));
\end{code}
\section{D registration}
\begin{code}
alias V = MuLLValue;
V[string] api;
api["Double"] = V((V[] args) {
    mullArity(args, 1);
    return V(args[0].number() * 2);
});
vm.registerLibrary("example", V(api));
\end{code}
Both versions use the same entry point at
\api{your-root/example/Package.mull}:
\begin{code}
return G:Native("example");
\end{code}

Validate argument counts before indexing, and use typed value accessors for
kind checks. Throw \api{mull::Error} or \api{MuLLError} for domain errors.
The interpreter attaches the calling expression's location when needed.
Callbacks may retain host state, so document the state's lifetime and sharing
rules. Wrapping a C function follows the same pattern: convert checked MuLL
values to the C signature, call it, and convert its result back.
